  "thought": "Beginning a read-only review of the Desktop-to-canonical WASM diff qualification.\n\nThe review will be in English under 650 words. AGENTS.md's Traditional Chinese rule is overridden by the explicit instruction. No tools or changes will be used.\n\nThe review covers the probe, benchmark, results, and rollout plan. The user wants continued integration with Grok/Muse. No independently installable artifact exists yet; the remote manifest is private0.1.0/workspace util with no published releases. Source build must not be conflated with a published artifact. A GitHub tarball was fetched at commit 912c8f92c4d66d0ad8a2887251c3325bbf8b5718. Upstream was built unchanged in a separate temp directory using a frozen filtered install and a rustup stable lib path workaround. An explicit module test was performed.\n\nDesktop source and dependencies remain unchanged. Pierre's processFile returns full metadata consumed by FileDiff without a second diff computation. The probe validates 215 fixtures for exact full metadata equality, full forwards, and 411 subset or complement single-revert outputs. Canonical validation still applies. Browser upstream smoke passed real Chromium CSP, cancellation, timeouts, and restart scenarios. Source CI diff job passed; main CI failed on a separate braces scan.\n\nThe review targets concrete correctness gaps in probe logic, coordinate mapping, conclusions, and rollout. The proposed rollout stages an initial artifact delivery, a shared async result, a unified snapshot for render, navigation, and actions, bounded scheduling with revision discard, explicit limits and fallback, and packaged tests. Performance is synthetic and noisy on Node only; no universal speed claim is made. Requiring publication does not authorize publishing. Adapter coordinate conversion must be distinguished from copying a diff kernel.\n\nThe 215 fixtures break down into 81 atomic edge cases, 14 newline and gap variants, and 120 seeded random samples. The atom set covers empty files, missing final newlines, CRLF, lone CR, CJK plus emoji, and null bytes. One gap remains: no systematic test mixes files without a final newline with multi-hunk diffs.\n\nThe seeded random generator uses a 32-bit JavaScript xorshift. Overflow into the sign bit is harmless because modulo zero masking keeps the sequence reproducible.\n\nGap fixtures probe hunk merging thresholds at indices 5 and 6 plus gap. Gaps of 1, 4, 7, 8, 9, 12, and 30 test whether adjacent hunks split or merge. This aligns with Pierre's typical three-line context window.\n\nSeeded fixtures range from 40 to 139 lines. Each contains four random splices of zero to three deletions and zero to three insertions.\n\nSeeded splices may produce identity diffs when all edits are no-ops or cancel out. This is acceptable.\n\nProbe coverage remains thin beyond roughly 140 lines. Binary and invalid UTF-8 files are absent. The lone CR atom covers carriage-return-only files.\n\nThe patch adapter joins hunk lines with `\\n` unconditionally. If canonical hunk lines already contain newlines or use `\\r\\n`, the resulting patch text will be corrupted. This is a critical correctness gap.\n\nOther probe coverage gaps include rename and file-mode diffs, multiple files, context line count variations, pure insertions at the start or deletions at the end, and Unicode normalization beyond emoji.\n\nThe `patchText` function builds patch strings from canonical hunks using `oldStart`, `oldLines`, `newStart`, `newLines`, and `lines`. It joins hunk lines with `\\n` unconditionally. This confirms the critical gap: if canonical hunk lines already contain newlines or use `\\r\\n`, the resulting patch text will be corrupted.\n\nThe adapter's `start - 1` conversion for empty ranges is correct under unified diff convention. A zero-based start with count zero is unlikely.\n\nThe probe never hit a zero-based start with empty count, so the adapter's `start - 1` conversion remains safe. Canonical hunk lines that already contain trailing newlines would be double-joined by the adapter's `\\n` join.\n\nThe probe's exact metadata equality check confirms canonical hunk lines lack embedded newlines. The adapter's unconditional `\\n` join is therefore safe for these fixtures.\n\nThe `\\ No newline at end of file` marker is not a correctness gap. Pierre's `processFile` infers newline-at-eof from the actual `oldFile` and `newFile` contents, so metadata equality holds even if canonical hunk lines omit the marker.\n\nPierre reconstructs rich metadata by applying the patch against the original files. The 215 identical full metadata results therefore strongly confirm the adapter's patch was accepted and produced the same structure Pierre would compute from the raw files alone.\n\nA caveat remains: `isDeepStrictEqual` compares objects deeply and may be sensitive to key order, omitted fields, or functions/undefined values that JSON omits.\n\nNode's `assert.deepStrictEqual` does not require key order for plain objects. It compares only enumerable properties and ignores prototype chains. Key order is compared for Maps instead.\n\nDeep equality of Pierre objects is safe because FileDiff depends on own data fields, not prototype methods. Circular structures and sparse arrays are unlikely. The probe excludes one hunk at a time to test subset and complement coordinate mapping.\n\nThe probe reconstructs each subset by shifting `newStart` to `oldStart + delta`, where delta accumulates line count differences across prior hunks. It then asserts that the engine’s `applyHunks` output matches the adapter’s `reject`-based revert. When spans are identical, it also checks that the result equals Pierre’s own revert.\n\nThe probe only rejects single hunks, not arbitrary subsets or multi-rejects. The coordinate adaptation formula `newStart = oldStart + delta` is being verified against canonical old-file and new-file coordinates.\n\nThe engine likely validates `newStart` against the running output position while applying a subset. Because the complement is applied to the original snapshot rather than the full `after`, the shifted `newStart` values may be incorrect.\n\nRejected hunks keep the old lines and discard the new ones. The output cursor therefore advances by oldLines rather than newLines. This subtle correction is needed to verify whether the shifted newStart values remain valid when a hunk is excluded.\n\nThe adapter rewrites each included hunk’s `newStart` as `oldStart + delta`, where delta accumulates line changes from prior hunks. The probe now questions whether this formula holds for a full diff, and whether the canonical engine validates `newStart` against the running output position.\n\nThe `newStart = oldStart + delta` formula is being refined. It is correct for the first hunk when unchanged prefixes align, but gaps between hunks may require context alignment beyond simple cumulative line changes.\n\nThe coordinate adaptation formula is confirmed correct. Inter-hunk lines are identical by definition, so the cumulative delta from prior included hunks properly shifts `newStart` for each subsequent hunk.\n\nThe probe spreads each included hunk and overrides only `newStart`, leaving the full `lines` array intact. The excluded hunk is omitted entirely. This correctly applies the subset while preserving the original line content.\n\nThe probe flags a potential coordinate underflow. If early hunks delete more lines than they add, the cumulative delta becomes negative. Then `newStart = oldStart + delta` can drop to zero or below, even though `oldStart` is at least one.\n\nThe coordinate underflow concern is resolved. Because canonical hunks are valid, the adjusted `newStart` values remain within bounds and pass all probe cases.\n\nThe probe never tests multi-hunk rejection, out-of-order exclusion, or line-level accept/reject within a hunk. It also omits the \"accept\" direction and overlapping hunk interactions.\n\nThe 411 single-exclude cases are fixture-index pairs, not 411 distinct fixtures. Identical-file fixtures with zero hunks contribute no cases, so the total is drawn from fixtures that actually contain hunks.\n\nThe probe only compares Pierre’s revert to the reconstructed output when spans match. Because identicalSpans and identicalFullMetadata both reached 215, the mismatch branch never triggered. The 215 sameReverts counts therefore reflect full-fixture results, not per-subset comparisons.\n\nIdentical-file fixtures count as sameReverts true vacuously, since the probe never enters their loops. The empty failures array confirms no renderer revert diverged from canonical applyHunks.\n\nThe sameReverts check is redundant. Because the adapted and Pierre objects are deep-equal, diffAcceptRejectHunk must return the same result for both unless the function depends on object identity, prototypes, or non-enumerable state.\n\nThe `fullForwardRoundTrips` counter is redundant. Every result record is pushed only after a successful forward apply and all subset assertions, so it simply counts completed fixtures.\n\nThe probe hardcodes two rejection assertions as true. It validates that stale context triggers a mismatch error, and that an aborted request followed by a fresh call succeeds.\n\nThe hardcoded rejection assertions sit in an outer try/finally block. If they throw, the exception escapes uncaught and the report is never printed.\n\nThe hardcoded rejection flags are only written after the asserts succeed, so their true values confirm the assertions passed. The regex `/mismatched/` is too loose to prove the exact failure mode; it could match any error message containing that substring.\n\nThe abort test only covers pre-aborted signals. It does not exercise abort-during-compute, timeouts, or restarts; those are claimed to be covered by separate browser smoke tests.\n\nThe probe also omits Worker recovery tests for limit exceeded and subsequent diff success.\n\nThe benchmark hashes only the engine JavaScript file, not the WASM binary if it is loaded separately.\n\nThe engine hash only covers the JavaScript loader, not the WASM blob or build inputs. The commit is stated in prose but never bound inside the report, so a different WASM next to the same JS would be hashed identically. Qualification identity is incomplete unless node.js inlines the WASM.\n\nThe Pierre version is read from node_modules but omitted from the report. Reproducibility is therefore unclear: which @pierre/diffs version produced the metadata equality?\n\nThe report omits the Pierre version even if it is pinned in a lockfile, so reviewers cannot verify the exact dependency. The process exit code is set before logging, and sequential awaits prevent race conditions. Span equality uses JSON.stringify rather than deepStrictEqual, but span tuples are simple number arrays, so this is acceptable.\n\nThe metadata equality check is fragile. Pierre objects may carry non-deterministic fields such as timestamps or IDs that could break the oracle later, though none appeared in these fixtures.\n\nThe adapter assumes canonical hunks are ordered and non-overlapping. It does not explicitly assert monotonicity, but apply would fail if the order were violated, so this is implicitly tested.\n\nThe probe never exercises the accept direction. If actions include both accept and reject, only reject complements are qualified.\n\n`additionLines.join(\"\")` assumes raw lines already contain their newlines.\n\nThe `additionLines.join(\"\")` assumption is safe. The probe asserts that the joined additions equal the reconstructed file, and that the full forward apply equals the original file. Because both assertions passed, the line separators are consistent between canonical and adapter.\n\nThe main methodological gap is the lack of an independent oracle for revert cases. The probe compares canonical apply against Pierre's reject, not a third expected string. If both implementations share a systematic off-by-one error, the probe would miss it. Full forward diffs have an independent oracle in `after`, but reverts rely solely on cross-checking Pierre against canonical apply.\n\nThe revert check is actually a valid oracle. Because the adapter deep-equals its metadata to Pierre's own diff, comparing canonical apply of the complement against Pierre's reject of that same diff is equivalent to checking whether the coordinate rewrite matches Pierre's current semantics. If the product requirement is simply to match Desktop's existing FileDiff behavior, this is sufficient for a consumer prototype.\n\nThe adapter joins patch lines with `\\n` and appends a trailing `\\n` after each hunk. It assumes canonical `hunk.lines` entries contain the leading prefix but not the trailing newline. The probe fixtures use `\\r\\n` line endings, so the adapter may misrepresent CRLF content unless canonical normalizes to LF.\n\nFull metadata equality confirms CRLF line endings were handled consistently between processFile and parseDiffFromFile. The probe passed on mixed endings such as `\\r` atoms.\n\nThe adapter omits the `\\ No newline at end of file` marker when emitting patches. This is acceptable because processFile trusts the provided file contents for line splitting and uses hunks only for change spans. Equality with parseDiffFromFile supports that the patch was sufficient.\n\nHeader `@@` lines omit function context. This is fine.\n\nEmpty hunks produce a minimal patch header and pass the diagonal atom. File renames are out of scope. The benchmark machine was saturated with load averages near 45, so the seven alternating warm samples are noisy and unstable.\n\nThe 50k/200 hunk case hit a resource limit and was rejected, so no comparison exists. Several WASM medians are faster than Pierre on smaller fixtures, but the saturated machine makes those differences noise or garbage-collection effects. The 1k cases are slightly slower. These numbers should not be used to justify the migration on performance. The key product finding is that large dense diffs fail closed and the worker recovers.\n\nThe probe validates full metadata against Pierre before timing begins. The benchmark extends that coverage to large CRLF files for cases that avoid the resource limit, so the 1k–50k fixtures matched Pierre on those as well. A bug in the benchmark error handling was noted but not detailed.\n\nThe benchmark catches limit errors but does not verify the error message actually contains that string. A metadata mismatch could theoretically trigger the same catch block, though this is unlikely. After the limit is hit, the worker recovers by processing a small subsequent diff.\n\nThe ~30 ms cold-start measurement does not isolate WASM compilation time. The metadata equality assertion runs outside the performance timer, so it cannot inflate the benchmark results. Alternating sample order reduces directional bias.\n\nThe benchmark median is the middle value of seven sorted samples. Variance is not reported, so load 45 may still skew the results. The comparison between canonical diff plus patch serialization and Pierre's parseDiffFromFile is the correct product-level check if the architecture keeps canonical diff generation separate from Pierre's render metadata projection.\n\nThe benchmark is not a pure diff-algorithm comparison. It is fair because patchText cost lives inside wasmAndMetadata, matching the proposed architecture.\n\nThe inferred conclusions are:\n- Qualification runs against a fixed commit via temporary build, not a published package.\n- processFile consumes canonical patches and file pairs to produce FileDiff metadata without a second diff.\n- All 215 fixtures pass full metadata equality, forward diffs work, and single-hunk reject coordinates match.\n- Stale context is rejected, pre-abort behavior works, and the worker recovers for the next call.\n\nThe probe and benchmark do not cover browser smoke tests, CSP, timeouts, worker restarts, or braces CI. Performance should not drive the migration decision.\n\nThe rollout order is: publish an artifact first, then share async results across render, navigation, and actions using a single snapshot, bound scheduling and revision discard, enforce explicit limits with fallback, and package tests.\n\nQualification is sufficient for a consumer prototype, but only if that prototype consumes a pinned published artifact rather than the temporary build.\n\nThe prototype should not import the temporary build as a sibling dependency. It must wait for or mock the published artifact interface instead.\n\nThe qualification justifies the adapter-plus-processFile approach, not production shipping. A behind-a-flag fixture-level prototype is valid once the artifact exists or an injected engine interface with the same contract is available.\n\nThe main blocker to production is the missing installable package. The private 0.1.0 manifest is not a release, and the temporary tarball build is not a dependency. CI must install the published artifact directly rather than rebuilding from a floating git snapshot. Publication is a gate, not an approval to publish now.\n\nThe hash covers only one JavaScript file, not the WASM binary or package integrity. The reproduction report must include the commit, the WASM hash, and the Pierre version.\n\nProbe coverage is narrower than production needs. It tests single-hunk reject only. Arbitrary accept or reject sets and line-level hunk splits are untested. There is no independent revert oracle beyond agreement with Pierre. Hardcoded booleans remain. Abort behavior is limited to pre-aborted state; in-flight cancellation, timeouts, and worker crashes are absent. Resource limit behavior appears in only one benchmark point. Binary diffs, renames, and file-mode changes are excluded from the equality suite.\n\nThe coordinate adapter is probe-only and must not become a forked diff kernel in Desktop. It needs a formal boundary test for the empty-range header rule: when count is zero, start becomes one less, including the edge case where start is one. The product still lacks a defined fallback for dense diffs that hit the resource limit; production must prevent UI spinning or partial metadata.\n\nThe probe uses a single synchronous before/after pair, so it does not exercise generation tokens, cancellation of superseded requests, or sharing one result across render, hunk navigation, and accept/reject. These rollout items are unproven and block production. Worker concurrency, transfer cost, and multiple open files are also untested.\n\nThe bounded queue and discard design is not proven by the probe. The main CI braces scan failure is unrelated to the diff job, so a passing diff does not mean the branch is merge-ready. Desktop dependencies must remain unchanged; the prototype should not import canonical source as a git dependency. The probe asserts isPartial false, but partial application paths for stale files or fuzzy context are not yet productized.\n\nThe prototype must define partial metadata behavior if FileDiff ever renders it. The qualification build uses a frozen filtered install with ignored scripts, but production requires provenance, signature, or registry authentication and exact version pinning per ADR-022; publishing is deferred to this review. A duplicate diff risk remains: any code path that still calls parseDiffFromFile for the same view would compute twice.\n\nThe probe does not prove that every FileDiff entry point will use a single snapshot. The prototype must inventory actual call sites rather than assume one consumer exists.\n\nA potential off-by-one bug in the newStart rewrite for accept versus reject operations still needs verification.\n\nThe newStart rewrite formula holds for accept and reject operations. A rejected hunk keeps its original start index. An included hunk advances by the prior delta contribution. The three-hunk case confirms the middle hunk’s start index ignores the skipped hunk.\n\nRejected hunks preserve their original output length, so the delta for subsequent hunks correctly counts only included hunks. Context lines inside a hunk are part of oldLines and newLines, and the net change is purely add or delete.\n\nAbsolute positions inside hunk lines would be stale after newStart overrides, but the content-based lines are safe and apply succeeded. The sameReverts flag starts false, so if spans differ it stays false even when adapter and Pierre match; this was not triggered.\n\nThe metadata counters are unaffected by fixture failures because the script still exits with code 1. The atom-based test suite covers 81 newline combinations, but it does not exercise a multi-line no-newline-at-eof case beyond atoms. The 120-sample xorshift seed is sufficient for prototype validation, yet it is not a proof of parser equivalence. The `parseDiffFromFile` call passes `true` as a fourth argument, which likely controls whether unchanged lines are included.\n\nThe probe treats its own output as the oracle. It does not verify that the fourth argument to `parseDiffFromFile` matches the options FileDiff actually passes. This leaves a concrete review gap: the probe never calls FileDiff or checks the option against the component.\n\nThe probe does not assert that processFile options match the production FileDiff call. If FileDiff passes different arguments for context lines, theme, syntax, or word-diff, the oracle will be wrong. Deep equality against parseDiffFromFile(..., undefined, true) is only valid if FileDiff builds its metadata from the same base snapshot.\n\nThe probe does not bind the user's claim that processFile produces FileDiff's consumed metadata to a component test. Browser CSP is absent from the files and is not evidenced by this review.\n\nThe rollout sequence is sound, and the first artifact delivery correctly gates production.\n\nThe prototype must introduce a request id so render, navigation, and actions can share one async diff result. The probe is synchronous and per-call, so it does not prove that hunk indices remain stable across scroll or next-diff operations.\n\nThe probe’s 215 identical spans prove that hunk indices align when metadata is deep-equal. Navigation and apply should therefore share one source of truth, such as processFile output for both. The risk is two sources of truth if navigation reads adapted.hunks while apply uses preview.hunks.\n\nProduction must not keep two separate hunk lists and assume their indices will stay equal. It should derive both UI indices and apply input from a single hunk list, or from that list plus a revision id.\n\nBounded scheduling and revision discard are untested. Production cannot safely support multi-file UI until it proves stale responses are discarded before they reach a new revision.\n\nThe benchmark confirms the resource limit is real and recoverable. Production must surface this limit to the user.\n\nA fallback to Pierre parseDiffFromFile on limit would reintroduce a second kernel on the hot failure path. It is acceptable only if labeled and tested, but silent fallback that can disagree with canonical on the cases that exceed limits is a correctness gap because those are exactly the cases that were not cross-checked.\n\nThe probe is an external script with a relative path into the desktop workspace. This creates workspace-layout coupling rather than a packaged test. It will not run in CI as-is unless checked in and pointed at the artifact.\n\nThe probe does not reimplement Myers, patience, or histogram kernels. It only rewrites empty-range unified header starts and recomputes newStart for partial apply. That coordinate conversion is still validated by the canonical engine. PatchText line joining and header synthesis must remain a pure encoding of canonical hunks to avoid kernel creep.\n\nThe rollout must explicitly forbid reconciliation on span mismatch. If spans diverge, the request should fail rather than fall back to Pierre. The probe already halts on assertion errors and contains no reconciliation logic.\n\nThe newStart rewrite is a standard incremental line-map for a subset, not a diff algorithm. This distinction must be made clear in the review.\n\nThe adapter should delegate subset application to the canonical engine by hunk index rather than computing newStart manually. This removes coordinate math from Desktop and reduces bugs. If that API is unavailable, the formula must be specified and tested for multi-reject, leading or trailing empty ranges, and net-negative deltas.\n\nThe missing engine-side subset API is a design note, not a prototype blocker, provided the newStart formula lives in one tested module. The off-by-one concern for empty ranges is resolved: patchText converts empty ranges for processFile, but the subset path bypasses it and passes hunks to applyHunks with rewritten newStart. Because oldLines=0 yields newStart = oldStart + delta, no subtraction is needed; that offset is a patch-header convention, not an engine coordinate convention.\n\nThe prototype should isolate the two coordinate conversions into separately named functions. Tests must verify that a zero-length hunk round-trips through both paths.\n\nDeep equality of the full metadata object is stronger than span equality. It covers Pierre fields such as tokens or decorations. The 215 full deep-equal matches are therefore the strongest result in the report.\n\n`isDeepStrictEqual` ignores prototype methods, so it can return true for objects with different prototypes but identical fields. FileDiff likely needs a real class instance rather than a plain structural clone. Because `processFile` returns the actual instance and it is deep-equal to `parseDiffFromFile` output, the prototype’s enumerable data matches. The prototype should therefore use the `processFile` return value directly instead of `structuredClone`.\n\nA cross-worker transfer would discard methods and prototypes. The canonical worker returns only raw hunks. Pierre processFile must run on the renderer side that holds the FileDiff component, not inside the WASM worker. The benchmark already runs processFile synchronously in the same Node process after engine.diff.\n\nThe probe does not test structured clone of the full metadata object. Production must not assume the complete Pierre object is transferable across threads. The shared async result needs explicit definition of which objects cross realms versus remain inside a single JavaScript realm.\n\nCancellation is a concrete architecture gap. If processFile runs synchronously and heavily, aborting the worker will not cancel projection. The scheduling design must note this.\n\nThe benchmark limit case is correctly surfaced: workerRecoveredAfterLimit is true and the error string contains the full Error message.\n\nLoad is not a correctness issue.\n\nI will phrase the review as positive distinctions rather than contrastive negations. The temp build qualifies the commit, but a published package is a separate artifact. The header and newStart rewrite are coordinate conversion; the diff decision stays in the canonical engine.\n\nThe verdict will open the review: the build is sufficient for a narrow consumer prototype, but not for production. The draft will then cover evidence, probe gaps, coordinate mapping, benchmark results, rollout corrections, and production blockers. I will reply in English and keep the word count under 650.\n\nThe subset delta formula assumes 1-based coordinates. If the engine were 0-based, the empty-range header conversion would be wrong and processFile would diverge from Pierre. The 215 full deep-equal matches across all fixtures including empty files provide strong empirical support that the 1-based assumption holds.\n\nThe joined hunk lines match the expected output exactly, including the edge case of a final line without a trailing newline. The merge-threshold gap fixtures all matched, confirming that canonical and Pierre agreed on splitting those gaps even with CRLF line endings.\n\nThe gap-7 fixture sits exactly at the default context threshold of three lines. Both algorithms split it, so span equality is expected there. The real value of these tests is the line-ending and boundary-detail differences between the two sides, not the merge decision itself.\n\nThe probe omits gap 6, the exact merge threshold. Gaps 1, 4, 7, 8, 9, 12, and 30 are tested. Gap 0 is also absent. This is a minor coverage hole.\n\nRandom splice generation can create unsorted or overlapping edits. The diff algorithm must normalize these inputs. That stress test is valuable.\n\nThe 120-seed random probe is modest but adequate. Null-byte handling was verified: both parsers survive `\\0` atoms, so truncation is not a concern. The pasted summary omits per-fixture details, yet the user confirmed no failures and the code derives counts from the full results array, so the summary is trusted.\n\nThe fullForwardRoundTrips counter is tautological with the successful array length, but it is harmless. Hardcoded strict flags sit after assertions, so a printed report implies they passed; this is a minor smell if the report builder is moved later. Browser and CI failures are outside these files and do not invalidate the diff job or qualify the desktop app.\n\nThe 411 figure is one excluded hunk per non-empty hunk list, not 411 files. Each canonical complement apply matched Pierre reject. Stale apply and a pre-aborted signal failed as asserted, then a later diff returned; the printed true flags sit after those asserts.\n\nThe benchmark also deep-equals the resource-limit cases, including a 50k-line file with two hunks. It confirms the worker accepts a later call after a limit error.\n\nThe coordinate mapping is now fully traced. `patchText` rewrites unified header ranges only: a canonical 1-based empty range becomes `start - 1`, which is why an empty old file renders as `-0,0`. `applyHunks` still receives engine hunks. For a single whole-hunk reject, `newStart = oldStart + sum of included (newLines - oldLines)` is the line map of applying that subset to the original snapshot.\n\nUnchanged inter-hunk regions keep old anchors valid, so the engine still validates and applies. A span mismatch should fail the request rather than fall back to reconciliation. The prototype is viable in this shape. Inject the published module or a test double with the same contract. In the renderer, project metadata with `processFile` and pass that object to FileDiff. Use the same revision’s canonical hunk list for apply. Keep header encoding and newStart adjustment in two named adapters. Cover count zero on both paths.\n\nOn limit, abort, or mismatch, the prototype must show an explicit fallback. It must not vendor canonical source, import a sibling tree, or retune spans to force a match.\n\nProduction still needs a published artifact with pinned WASM bytes, package version, and Pierre version. The current report hashes only Node.js. Requiring a release is a gate, not permission to publish.\n\nThe probe never leaves Node. Electron CSP, in-flight cancel, timeout, and worker restart are outside these two files. The pre-abort check does not cover cancel during diff.\n\nThe probe never tests structured clone of the full Pierre metadata object. A shared async result must name its realms explicitly: canonical hunks can cross postMessage, but the Pierre object should be built where FileDiff runs.\n\nSnapshot identity is unproven under concurrency. There is no revision token, no discard of a late response, and no test that render, navigation, and actions read one generation. Span equality held here, yet production should not keep two live hunk lists that are only assumed to match.\n\nAction coverage stops at one whole-hunk reject.\n\nMulti-hunk rejects, accepts, and line-level rejects are untested. The revert oracle is Pierre plus the rewritten apply, which matches current FileDiff behavior but cannot catch a shared off-by-one error. An engine apply-by-index would remove the delta math from Desktop. The resource limit is a product hole: dense 50k-line input throws `Diff resource limit exceeded`, so any silent Pierre fallback on those unchecked cases can diverge; that fallback must be visible and tested. These scripts are not packaged tests.",
